This extension adds Red Dead Redemption 2 modding support to Vortex Mod Manager.

[b][color=#ff0000]Remove the official "Red Dead Redemption 2 Support" extension before using this one.[/color][/b] Both register the same game, so Vortex only runs one of them. Mods you installed with the official extension are moved over automatically.

[b]You must install manually: Use the Manual Download, then drag and drop the archive onto the Vortex Extensions view to install.[/b]

[b]⭐ Improvements Over the Official Extension:[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Lenny's Mod Loader (LML) support:[/b] LML mods install to their own folder in "lml", loose texture and model mods install to "lml\stream", and the LML Mod Manager app installs with the loader and can be launched from Vortex. Readme and instruction files from LML-only archives stay out of the game folder.[/*]
[*][b]Load Order page for LML mods:[/b] drag mods to set their order and tick or untick them to turn them on or off. Vortex saves this to LML's own mods.xml, so it stays in sync with LML Mod Manager both ways, and it works even if LML Mod Manager has never been opened.[/*]
[*][b]ScriptHookRDR2 V2 installs automatically[/b] from Nexus Mods, together with its ASI loader, and you get update notifications. The official extension only warned that ScriptHook was missing.[/*]
[*][b]ScriptHook, ASI plugins and the ASI loader install to the game folder.[/b] The official extension put ScriptHook in the "scripts" folder, where it does not work.[/*]
[*][b]OpenIV is no longer needed.[/b] The official extension deployed mods to "mods\source\content", where the game never loads them.[/*]
[*][b]ASI mods that also include an LML folder[/b] (such as Ambient Gang and Companion System) install both parts correctly.[/*]
[*][b].NET script mods install to the "scripts" folder[/b], and only real .NET scripts do. The official extension sent every mod made only of .dll files there, including ScriptHook and upscalers. If ScriptHookRDR2 .NET is missing, you get a notification with a download button.[/*]
[*][b]Save game and config mods install where the game reads them:[/b] save slots go to your save folder (never another player's profile settings), and "system.xml" goes to the game's Settings folder.[/*]
[*][b]Mods with several versions or optional parts[/b] in one archive (eye colours, editions, compatibility patches) ask which parts to install.[/*]
[*][b]Translations and compatibility files[/b] for a mod you already have install into that mod's folder and override its files, including translations published on their own Nexus Mods page (such as those for Shtivi's mods).[/*]
[*][b]Browse and install mods from RDR2Mods.com[/b] inside Vortex, plus a Visual Redemption download button with update notifications.[/*]
[*][b]Rockstar Games Launcher installs are detected[/b], not only Steam and Epic.[/*]
[/list]
[b]✅ Supported Versions:[/b]
[list]
[*]Steam[/*]
[*]Epic[/*]
[*]Rockstar Games Launcher[/*]
[*]Other versions may need to select the installation location manually.[/*]
[/list]
[b]🛠️ Mod Installation Notes:[/b]
[list]
[*][b][color=#ffff00]Automatically installs ScriptHookRDR2 V2 and its ASI loader from Nexus Mods[/color][/b] when they are not in the game folder already.[/*]
[*]Loose texture and model files (".ytd", ".ymt" and similar) without an install.xml install to the [b]"lml\stream"[/b] folder, where LML loads them.[/*]
[*]ASI mods that also ship an LML mod folder install the plugin to the game folder and the LML folder to [b]"lml"[/b].[/*]
[*]Archives With Several Versions or Optional Parts mods with two or more parts that each install on their own install to wherever each chosen part's own installer puts it.[/*]
[*]Add-on mods with files that replace files of an installed mod from the same Nexus Mods page install to that mod's folder, overriding its files.[/*]
[*]Lenny's Mod Loader installs to the game folder, recognised by a "vfs.asi" file.[/*]
[*]ScriptHookRDR2 mods with a "scripthookrdr2.dll" file install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Root Folder mods with a "x64", "RampageFiles" or "reshade-shaders" folder install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]ASI Loader installs to the game folder, recognised by a "dinput8.dll" or "version.dll" file.[/*]
[*]ASI Plugin mods install to the game folder.[/*]
[*].NET Scripts (ScriptHookRDR2 .NET) mods with a ".cs" or ".vb" or .NET ".dll" script install to `scripts`.[/*]
[*]LML mods with an "install.xml" file and a ".ytd", ".ydr", ".ydd", ".yft", ".ymt", ".ymap" or one of 7 other file types install to the "lml" folder.[/*]
[*]Config mods with a "system.xml" file install to the game's config folder.[/*]
[*]Save mods with a save slot file ("SRDR30000" to "SRDR30015") install to your profile folder.[/*]
[*]Any other mod not described above installs to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Archives with a FOMOD installer (a "fomod" folder with ModuleConfig.xml) are handed to Vortex's built-in FOMOD installer instead.[/*]
[/list]
[b]📋 Usage Notes:[/b]
[list]
[*]You can open several useful files/folders/URLs using the buttons within the folder icon on the Mods toolbar.[/*]
[*]You can browse RDR2Mods.com and install its mods directly in Vortex using the "Browse RDR2Mods" page in the sidebar.[/*]
[*]Lenny's Mod Loader is only published on RDR2Mods.com, which blocks automatic downloads. Use the "Download Lenny's Mod Loader" button to open its page in Vortex and click Download there.[/*]
[*]Launch the LML Mod Manager from the Vortex dashboard to choose the options of LML mods that have them.[/*]
[*]The game launches in offline mode (-scOfflineOnly) from Vortex, which keeps mods out of Red Dead Online.[/*]
[/list]
[b]📝 Notes for Mod Authors:[/b]
[spoiler]
Packaging rules for Red Dead Redemption 2 mods, so Vortex installs them to the right place.

Vortex decides what a mod is by looking at the files and folders inside the archive. It tries each installer in order and the first one that matches wins, so archive layout is what determines where your mod ends up.

========================================================================

Archives With Several Versions or Optional Parts:

When one archive holds alternatives (eye colours, editions, a manual and a mod manager version) or optional extras (compatibility patches), Vortex asks which parts to install. Each part you tick then goes through the installers below as if it were its own archive.

  Requirements:
    - A part is a folder that holds an "lml" folder, an "install.xml" mod folder, a "replace" or "stream" folder, or an ".asi", ".dll" or ".cs" file of its own.
    - With no such folder, folders that hold a file with the same name as a file in another folder are the alternatives ("Blue Eyes\horse.ytd" and "Brown Eyes\horse.ytd").
    - Only a part with its own "lml" (or "replace"/"stream") folder can be an optional extra inside another part. A plugin's sub folders and a wrapped "install.xml" folder belong to it.
    - The part at the top of the archive is ticked by default. Collections and other unattended installs take the default without asking.

  Installs to: wherever each chosen part's own installer puts it

  Common mistakes:
    - Name each alternative's folder after what it is ("Blue Eyes", "Lite Version") - the folder path is what the user sees in the list.
    - If every part is meant to be installed together, put them in one "lml" folder instead.

========================================================================

Add-ons (Translations and Compatibility Files):

A translation or compatibility file uploaded beside a mod on its Nexus Mods page installs into that mod's own folder, over the file it replaces. Vortex reads the Nexus mod id of the download and looks for an installed mod with the same id.

  Requirements:
    - Every file in the archive (readmes and images aside) must have the same name as a file of the installed mod, either at the same relative path or as the only file with that name. One unmatched file and the archive is installed by the other installers instead.
    - The add-on takes the installed mod's mod type and is deployed after it, so its files win.
    - An archive with a ".asi", ".dll" or ".exe" file or an "install.xml" is a main file or an update, never an add-on.

  Installs to: that mod's folder, overriding its files

  Common mistakes:
    - Upload the add-on on the same Nexus Mods page as the mod it belongs to, and keep the file names identical to the ones it replaces ("Lang.dat", "prompts.gxt2").
    - If the mod is not installed yet, the add-on installs by the usual rules, usually to the game folder. Install the mod first.

========================================================================

Lenny's Mod Loader:

Recognised when the archive contains a "vfs.asi" file.

  Installs to: the game folder

========================================================================

ScriptHookRDR2:

Recognised when the archive contains a "scripthookrdr2.dll" file.

  Installs to: the game folder

========================================================================

Root / Game Folder Mods:

For mods laid out the same way the files appear inside the game folder. Vortex copies the matched folder and everything under it straight into the game.

[code]
MyRootMod.zip
└── x64\
    └── ... files in their real relative locations
[/code]

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a folder named "x64", "RampageFiles" or "reshade-shaders" in the archive.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Zipping the folder that CONTAINS the game folders, instead of the game folders themselves, adds an extra level and misplaces every file.

========================================================================

ASI Loader:

Recognised when the archive contains a "dinput8.dll" or "version.dll" file.

  Installs to: the game folder

========================================================================

ASI Plugin:

Handled by the "testAsiPlugin" installer. Inspect the extension source for the exact archive layout it expects.

  Installs to: the game folder

========================================================================

.NET Scripts (ScriptHookRDR2 .NET):

Scripts for ScriptHookRDR2 .NET install to the game's "scripts" folder. The script needs Community ScriptHookRDR2 .NET; Vortex offers to download it when it is missing.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a ".cs" or ".vb" file, or a ".dll" that is a .NET assembly. A native ".dll" (an upscaler, ReShade) is not a script and installs to the game folder instead.
    - Files inside the archive's own "scripts" folder install from there; otherwise everything in the script's folder goes into "scripts", so ".ini" and ".xml" files stay beside it.
    - An archive that also has an ".exe", an "install.xml" or LML assets is not a script mod.

  Installs to: "scripts"

  Common mistakes:
    - Shipping a standalone tool (".exe" plus its .NET ".dll" files) - it is not installed to "scripts", which is right, but keep tools and scripts in separate archives.

========================================================================

LML Mod:

Recognised when the archive contains a "install.xml" file and a ".ytd", ".ydr", ".ydd", ".yft", ".ymt", ".ymap", ".ytyp", ".ybn", ".awc", ".meta", ".ymf", ".yld" or ".gxt2" file.

  Installs to: "lml"

========================================================================

Config File Mods:

Configuration tweaks, deployed to the game's config location.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by any file named "system.xml".

  Installs to: the game's config folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Shipping a config file with one of these names inside an unrelated mod makes the whole archive install as a config mod.

========================================================================

Save Game Files:

Save slots install straight into your profile folder in "Documents\Rockstar Games\Red Dead Redemption 2\Profiles".

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a file whose name starts with "SRDR3", including the ".bak" copies. They can sit anywhere in the archive (a profile id folder, a "Profiles" folder).
    - Only the save slots install. A shipped profile's "ProfileSettings", "cfg.dat" and other files are left out, so they never overwrite the user's own.
    - Needs the game to have been started once, so that the profile folder exists, and Documents on the same drive as the game and the Vortex staging folder. Otherwise the install stops with a message saying why.

  Installs to: your profile folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Two saves with the same slot number in one archive - only one of them installs. Use different slot numbers, or separate folders so Vortex asks which to install.

========================================================================

Fallback Installer:

The catch-all. Any archive that matched none of the installers above lands here and is copied across unchanged.

[b][color=#ffff00]NOTE: Landing in the fallback installer is a signal your archive layout needs fixing.[/color][/b]

  Requirements:
    - Reaching this installer usually means the archive was not laid out in a way Vortex recognised.
    - Vortex shows the user a notification when a mod installs through the fallback.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - If your mod lands here unintentionally, re-check the layouts above - users will see a fallback warning and may report the mod as broken.

========================================================================

Rules That Apply To Every Mod Type:

    - Archives that contain a FOMOD installer (a "fomod" folder with "ModuleConfig.xml") are handed to Vortex's built-in FOMOD installer instead, and none of the rules above apply.
    - Folder and file name matching is case-insensitive.
    - Extra wrapper folders around a recognised folder are generally fine; the installer searches at any depth.
[/spoiler]
